speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I would like to read into the record of the House the conflict of interest guidelines set down by the Prime Minister. There are two principles:
(1)	The onus for preventing real, apparent or foreseeable conflicts of interest rests with the individual;
In this case, Mr. Turner.
(2)	Ministers must perform and appear to perform their official responsibilities and arrange their private affairs in a manner that will conserve and enhance public confidence and trust in government and that will prevent conflicts of interest from arising;
Acceptance of that standard is the price that Ministers must pay for access to confidential documents about the business of the country. Will the Prime Minister explain to us why the standards of a future Prime Minister should be lower than the standards of a serving Minister?
